  "statement": "Use the Newton-Raphson method on $f(x)=x^{3}-2x-5$ starting from $x_{0}=2$ to find the real root, giving $x_{1}$, $x_{2}$ and the limiting value to 6 d.p.",
  "solution": "$f'(x)=3x^{2}-2$, so $x_{n+1}=x_{n}-\\dfrac{x_{n}^{3}-2x_{n}-5}{3x_{n}^{2}-2}$. $x_{1}=2-\\dfrac{8-4-5}{12-2}=2+\\tfrac{1}{10}=2.1$. $x_{2}=2.1-\\dfrac{9.261-4.2-5}{3(4.41)-2}=2.1-\\dfrac{0.061}{11.23}\\approx2.094568$. The root is approximately $2.094551$ (to 6 d.p.).",
  "hints": [
    "Compute $f'(x)=3x^{2}-2$ for the update formula.",
    "Run two iterations from $x_{0}=2$."
  ],
